  "slug": "infinite-entropy-xhj",
  "title": "Infinite Entropy",
  "summary": "An interview between Feral File and Rafaël Rozendaal ahead of the opening of his solo exhibition Infinite Entropy. \n\n<b>Is the piece “Infinite Entropy,” also the name of the exhibition, something that you created expressly for this show, or how did it come about?</b>\n\nI’d been making websites for 20 years, and I had so many domain names and server issues to manage that it stopped being fun. I liked making them, but I didn’t like publishing them. So I had a whole three years of unpublished works before NFTs came along, and all of a sudden it seemed to fit that very well.\n\nThis work in particular was something that I made for an exhibition called <a href=\"https://www.newrafael.com/permanent-distraction/\">Permanent Distraction</a> at Site Gallery in the UK. When the lockdown started, the exhibition was postponed for two years, and in the meantime we did an online exhibition. This work was published with some other works on the Site Gallery website, but it wasn’t in the final selection for Permanent Distraction. I kept playing with it and it never felt finished, but I think now it has really found its place. \n\n<b>This work has a softer composition than a lot of your other works. Why is that?</b>\n\nThe way I think visually is with lines — it’s very simple, and very quick; it’s similar to drawing diagrams. But for this piece I was drawing circles, and though it’s still the simple instruction of a circle, I also added a level of transparency that I hadn’t used before. The content of the circle becomes transparent over the spreading out, the further you get from the center. As with all of my works, this one was made with a bunch of small steps — the transparency was just one of those small steps, but it creates a very different visual result. \n\nWith this piece I also wanted to play with the idea of emptiness and things being filled up and busy. There’s constantly a tension between where you feel like there’s some space and where it feels very crowded. The script is simple...",
